meta {
  name: Update Profile Photo
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/v1/client/me
  body: multipartForm
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
  X-Locale: {{locale}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  _method: PATCH
  photo: @file()
  ~name: Merdan Annagurdow
  ~city_id: {{city_id}}
}

docs {
  # Update Profile Photo

  Same endpoint as **Update Profile** (`PATCH /client/me`), used whenever the
  edit carries the avatar.

  **Why `POST` + `_method: PATCH` instead of a real PATCH verb:**
  PHP only populates file uploads (`$_FILES`) for `POST` requests — a genuine
  `PATCH` with a `multipart/form-data` body arrives with the file silently
  dropped. Laravel's documented workaround is to send `POST` with a `_method`
  field spoofing the real verb; the router then dispatches it to the same
  `update` action as a `PATCH`. `name` and `city_id` may ride along.

  **Fields:**
  - `photo` — `jpeg|png|jpg|webp`, max 5 MB. Converted **inline** (no queue) to
    WebP and downscaled to **512 px wide**, height proportional; smaller images
    are not upscaled. The previous file is deleted.

  Omitting `photo` never blanks the stored avatar — it stays as it is.

  **Response 200:** `ClientProfileResource` with `photo` / `photo_url`

  **Errors:**
  - `422` — validation error (not an image, over 5 MB, city_id not found)
  - `401` — missing / invalid token
}
